  "mother": {
    "preparation": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "preparation",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Develop and measure a serial O(N²) kernel. With the existing matrix, initialize a signed 64-bit column-sum array for the first S rows. For each output top row, scan column sums with a horizontal S-wide sliding sum to find its best window, then update each column sum by subtracting the departing matrix row and adding the entering row. This replaces the source’s O(S(N−S+1)²) per-move work without a large prefix table. Preserve the full input/output contract and check correctness on all four baseline cases.",
        "B": "Explore parallelizing the O(N²) rolling-sum kernel across contiguous ranges of output top rows. Each worker should initialize private column sums for its first row, scan and update within its range, and contribute one local maximum; avoid per-window synchronization and large shared prefix arrays. The range starts duplicate O(SN) initialization per worker, so measure end-to-end and use a general size/work threshold or the serial path where parallel overhead or duplicated work outweighs gains. Use only parallel support already available in the supplied build/runtime; do not change the toolchain.",
        "C": "Investigate input and memory-layout costs independently: replace the vector-of-vectors and its zero-initialization/allocation overhead with a single row-major buffer, and consider a buffered signed-integer parser that handles whitespace and negative values. Avoid mixing input APIs. Measure the parser/layout change end-to-end, ideally both with the original kernel and with an O(N²) kernel, so any gain is attributable and remains compatible with every valid input. Do not assume I/O is the bottleneck without measurements.",
        "G": "Integrate the strongest correct measured candidates, starting with the best end-to-end result rather than assuming theoretical or additive gains. Benchmark each tree’s candidate and promising combinations under identical authorized conditions; integration can regress, so retain a combination only if its own measurement supports it. Check output against the oracle on all four available cases and preserve correctness for all 0<S<N<8000 inputs, signed matrix values, and the specified output format. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, or target, and do not specialize to test values.",
        "analysis": "The source is Problem B’s serpentine sliding-window implementation: each neighboring window updates S elements, giving roughly O(S(N−S+1)²) work after initialization. The main algorithmic opportunity is an O(N²) separable rolling-sum scan; the other plausible costs are input parsing and matrix storage. This run’s baseline is valid and correct on 4 cases; its three times are 0.1261676457, 0.1251775231, and 0.1259248648 seconds (mean 0.1257566779 seconds), with score 0.5. No case sizes or profiles are supplied, so the dominant cost is unknown. The target is ARM64 with 4 CPU cores and no GPU: prioritize measured CPU, memory, and I/O behavior, not an inferred test shape."
      },
